  <h2>Your entitlements</h2>
  <p>As a patient at St. Paul's Mission Hospital, you have the right to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Be treated with dignity and privacy at all times.</li>
    <li>
      Receive clear information about your diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis.
    </li>
    <li>Give or refuse consent for any procedure.</li>
    <li>Access your medical records, subject to applicable law.</li>
    <li>Request a second opinion or referral.</li>
    <li>Be informed of the cost of care before it is provided.</li>
    <li>
      Have a family member or trusted person present during care, where
      clinically appropriate.
    </li>
    <li>Practice your faith and receive spiritual support if you wish.</li>
    <li>Complain without fear of retaliation or reduced care.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The full <a href='/governance/patient-rights/'>Patient Rights Charter</a> details
    each of these.
  </p>

  <h2>Giving feedback or lodging a complaint</h2>
  <p>
    We welcome feedback — positive or negative. Every complaint is logged,
    investigated, and used to improve services. There are three ways to reach
    us:
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1 — Frontline resolution</h3>
  <p>
    Speak to the nurse-in-charge or department head at the point of service.
    Most concerns are resolved immediately at this level.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2 — Formal complaint</h3>
  <p>
    If not resolved, submit a written complaint to the Hospital Administrator's
    office or by email to <a href='mailto:info@spmh.co.ke'>info@spmh.co.ke</a>.
    You will receive an acknowledgment within 24 hours and a resolution within
    14 working days.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3 — External escalation</h3>
  <p>
    If you remain dissatisfied, you may escalate to the County Department of
    Health, the Kenya Medical Practitioners and Dentists Council, or the
    Catholic Diocese of Homa Bay.
  </p>

